{"kind":"assert","text":"The solo-adoption gap this request describes is smaller than stated, and measuring it changes what the work should be.\n\nThe request says every entry path starts from an empty directory and that nothing covers adopting gitseq alone, \"which reads as a precondition that the organization must agree first.\" docs/getting-started.md already says the opposite, and already said it three hours before the request was filed. Its summary line reads \"create a workroom in a repository you already have\". Line 29 opens the workroom section with \"A workroom is an overlay on a repository you already have — yours, not gitseq's.\" Line 73 states \"It is safe to run inside a repository you are working in. It writes no branch, moves no HEAD, and touches neither the index nor the working tree.\" That text is in commit 01d20a8e at 2026-08-10 07:57; the request was filed at 11:04.\n\nThe mktemp -d on line 41 of that page is not a precondition. It is the runnable example the documentation gate requires, sitting directly under the same command written against a real path in a `text` block. internal/docset/examples_test.go executes every `sh` block against a scratch workroom, so a page that shows a real path must show a scratch one too. Reading those as \"you must start from an empty directory\" reads the harness as the instruction.\n\nWhat is genuinely missing is one level up. README.md never tells the reader they can adopt this alone. Its Getting Started section says only \"Follow Getting started for first-time initialization\" and warns that gs init \"is a bit of a chore\" — so a reader deciding whether gitseq applies to them never learns, from the page they actually land on, that it overlays a repository they already work in and needs nothing from their organization. The gap is a missing sentence in the README, not a missing page.\n\nSo I am not adding a how-to that would restate getting-started.md. Duplicating a page that is already correct is how two sources of truth start, and the second one is the one that goes stale. Scope I will implement: state the job in hugh's framing at the top of README.md, say plainly there that adoption is solo and points at the existing page, and restore the standing prompt to hugh's exact words. The prompt defect is real and unchanged — README line 26 paraphrases it as \"Using the gitseq MCP ... to keep progressing\" where the words are \"Using the gitseq mcp server ... to keep work progressing.\"\n\nRecording this because it narrows a condition of satisfaction I promised against, and because the next person to read the request should not go looking for a gap that closed before it was written."}